<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Afghanistan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Afghanistan is a mountainous region of Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a> lying between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>. Its passes made it a key corridor for armies and trade, and it was contested or crossed by many empires, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a> to the Khwarazmian and Chagatai realms. In later centuries it was the base of Afghan power that confronted the Afsharid and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> empires, and a buffer between the Russian and British empires.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/afghanistan.jpg" alt="Afghanistan" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Taliban Vector: Lexicon · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Taliban.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The empire's collapse after 1747 created the conditions for the emergence of an independent Afghan state: Ahmad Shah Durrani, formerly commander of Nader's Abdali Afghan contingent, was chosen as ruler at Kandahar within months of Nader's death, founding the Durrani Empire that became the basis of modern <b>Afghanistan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>By 2021 the armed forces had 126,000 active personnel and 17,000 paramilitary troops, a defence budget of $2.24 billion in 2020, 5.4 percent of GDP, and an arms industry of its own; Azerbaijan cooperates with NATO through the Partnership for Peace and sent peacekeepers to Iraq and <b>Afghanistan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>A founding member of NATO in 1949, Canada fought in the Korean War, joined the United States in the North American Air Defence Command in 1958, took part in many United Nations peacekeeping missions, and fought in <b>Afghanistan</b> from 2001.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canada">Canada · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The khanate's Islamisation, culminating in Tarmashirin's conversion and confirmed in the east by Tughlugh Timur's conversion around 1354, helped fix the religious character of Central Asia as a Muslim region, with profound and long-lasting consequences from <b>Afghanistan</b> to the Tarim Basin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Australia fought in Korea, Malaya and Vietnam, where national servicemen served from 1966, led the international force in East Timor in 1999 and joined the wars in <b>Afghanistan</b> and Iraq.</blockquote><a href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Ahmad then turned west to Mashhad, whose ruler Shahrukh Afshar acknowledged Afghan suzerainty, and sent an army north of the Hindu Kush that brought the Tajik, Hazara, Uzbek, Turkmen and other peoples of northern <b>Afghanistan</b> under his control.</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
